window.docContents[179].resolve({"/Connectivity/#chapter-connectivity":{"contents":"variable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nopen Walk\n\n\nA graph is connected if it has at least one vertex and there is a walk between\nevery pair of vertices.\n\nIn this chapter we study graph connectivity. Building on the reachability relation of\nReachability, we start with Mathlib's notions Preconnected and\nConnected, connected components and bridges, and show that the Mycielskian mμ[G]\nis connected iff G has no isolated vertices. We then look at trees, and show that\nthe infinite path pathNat₁ is one. We show that a graph is bipartite iff it has no\ncycle of odd length, and that in a graph of maximum degree at most 2 every cycle\nruns through its whole component. We prove Euler's theorem: a finite graph with at\nleast one vertex has an Eulerian trail iff all its edges lie in one component and it\nhas either no vertex of odd degree or exactly two. We then turn to Mathlib's edge\nconnectivity (IsEdgeConnected), define vertex connectivity (IsVertexConnected),\nand prove that a k-vertex-connected graph is k-edge-connected and that the\nn-dimensional hypercube Q[n] is both. This gives the inequality κ(G) ≤ λ(G)\nbetween the vertex and edge connectivity numbers.\n\nThe final two sections are more advanced. We prove Brooks' theorem: a finite graph\nof maximum degree at most Δ is Δ-colourable unless it contains a\n(Δ + 1)-clique or (when Δ = 2) one of its components is an odd cycle. Then we\ndefine the blocks of a graph as the classes of an equivalence relation on edges,\nbuilding our own quotient type just as Mathlib builds ConnectedComponent, and prove\nthat, for n ≥ 1, a finite graph is n-colourable if each of its blocks is.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"10. Connectivity","id":"/Connectivity/#chapter-connectivity"}});